<clickhouse>
    <!-- Local overlay for Opik's ClickHouse. Not part of upstream comet-ml/opik.

         Why this exists: the `system` database grew to 12.15 GiB / 1.48 billion rows in
         35 days, while the `opik` database (the actual traces) held only 55.80 MiB - a
         ratio of 223:1. Root cause was a 1-second healthcheck generating 173k error log
         lines per day, combined with Trace/Debug log level and no TTL on 9 of 13 system
         tables. See the repository README for the full incident write-up.

         Installed into the `opik-opik_clickhouse-config` Docker volume, so it survives
         both `git pull` in the upstream clone and container recreation (Opik's
         clickhouse-init only copies files in, it never removes unknown ones).

         NOTE when editing: XML comments must not contain two consecutive hyphens. -->

    <!-- 1. metric_log: force Compact parts BEFORE attaching a TTL.

         This table has 1552 columns. TTLDropMerge and TTLDeleteMerge are ALWAYS forced
         to the Horizontal algorithm (the `vertical_merge_optimize_ttl_delete` setting
         that would allow otherwise does not exist in 26.3), and a Horizontal merge over
         Wide parts must open one write buffer per column - roughly 3.6 GiB per merge.
         Attaching a TTL without these two settings recreates exactly the merge deadlock
         that filled 259 GB of disk on the SigNoz stack.

         Compact parts keep all 1552 columns in a single file, so Horizontal stays cheap:
         measured 122 MiB instead of 3.58 GiB. The thresholds only need to exceed
         anything a 14-day TTL can accumulate, i.e. effectively "never Wide".

         Trade-off: reading a single column from a Compact part decompresses the shared
         block, so column-selective queries on metric_log are slower. Nothing queries
         this table, so that cost is not paid in practice.

         Declared via <engine> because SETTINGS can only be given inside it; the TTL must
         therefore live in the engine string too. Do not also add a separate <ttl> tag -
         ClickHouse refuses to start when both are present. -->
    <metric_log>
        <engine>ENGINE = MergeTree
                PARTITION BY toYYYYMM(event_date)
                ORDER BY (event_date, event_time)
                TTL event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE
                SETTINGS min_bytes_for_wide_part = 10737418240,
                         min_rows_for_wide_part = 100000000</engine>
    </metric_log>

    <!-- 2. A 14-day TTL for the remaining system log tables. These are all narrow (33
         columns at most), so their Horizontal TTL merges are already cheap - measured
         on this host at 15 to 265 MiB with zero failures. -->
    <trace_log>               <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl> </trace_log>
    <text_log>                <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl> </text_log>
    <part_log>                <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl> </part_log>
    <query_log>               <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl> </query_log>
    <error_log>               <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl> </error_log>
    <asynchronous_metric_log> <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl> </asynchronous_metric_log>
    <query_metric_log>        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl> </query_metric_log>
</clickhouse>
